  "worked_solution": "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-2$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-5 & =-2(x+1) \\\\ y-5 & =-2x-2 \\\\ y & =-2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
